1) Luck:
One increases one's luck by offering fresh corpses to one's god (especially
unicorns of other alignments). One decreases one's luck by making one's
god angry (e.g. by praying too often). Luck (whether bad or good) eventually
times out if not sustained by repeatedly performing "lucky" or "unlucky"
actions. Carrying a luckstone ensures that luck (good or bad) does not
time out. Carrying a blessed (cursed) luckstone ensures that good (bad)
luck does not time out, and gives extra (reduced) luck.
Luck affects the outcome of various actions. For example, the probability
of succeeding at casting a spell of teleportation when falling into water
(if the spell is known) increases with luck. Luck also affects your relation 
with your god and the amount of help you will get by praying (from getting
your worst problem fixed to getting a gratuitous favor, such as a new
intrinsic). So it's really worth spending time improving your luck and
even wishing for a blessed luckstone if you haven't got one.  

2) The Hand of Elbereth:
If you are in very good standing with your god (after sacrificing a lot to
him/her) and if your luck is high, praying to your god might make you "The
Hand of Elbereth" (or Arioch for chaotic characters). Besides giving you a
fancy title, this gives you the following intrinsics if you didn't have them
already: see invisible, fire resistance, cold resistance, poison resistance.
In addition, if you are chaotic, Stormbringer will appear at your feet.

3) The Moon:
Well, the only thing I was able to dig out from the code is that on a full
moon, #(c)hatting with your dog will result in it howling at night, instead
of barking, yipping, etc... The spoilers say that full moon/new moon
affect some monsters (presumably weremonsters) but I wasn't able to
determine how.

